  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "What type of quadratic curve is defined by the following equation?\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{17}{4}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we complete the squares for the variables ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} & =-\\frac{17}{4} \\\\ ({x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1})+({x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{2}) & =-\\frac{17}{4} \\\\ ({x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2})+({x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}) & =-\\frac{17}{4} \\\\ ({x}_{1}+2)^{2}-{2}^{2}+{({x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =-\\frac{17}{4} \\\\ ({x}_{1}+2)^{2}-4+{({x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4} & =-\\frac{17}{4} \\\\ ({x}_{1}+2)^{2}+{({x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nA sum of squares is equal to zero if and only if each summand is zero, so the given quadratic equation represents the single point $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2})$ in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.",
